{"title":"1993 AHSME Problems/Problem 25","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/4/2245ad48a9140cd87641bc1cc671f81e0d1b6803.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((0,0)--(1,sqrt(3)),black+linewidth(.75),EndArrow); draw((0,0)--(1,-sqrt(3)),black+linewidth(.75),EndArrow); draw((0,0)--(1,0),dashed+black+linewidth(.75)); dot((1,0)); MP(&quot;P&quot;,(1,0),E); [/asy]\" width=\"95\" height=\"252\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> be the set of points on the rays forming the sides of a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/4/9b49fcb2f74ccae5ec0e722a8c65b5b9bbd93720.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$120^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> angle, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be a fixed point inside the angle \non the angle bisector. Consider all distinct equilateral triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/c/91c587b0c1e5de0bde79b9d8c36913c16dee04d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PQR$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"16\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />. \n(Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> may be on the same ray, and switching the names of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> does not create a distinct triangle.) \nThere are\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/6/a46b502d322234f1d94cb3e7cddb1b7675af9afd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A) exactly 2 such triangles} \\quad\\\\ \\text{(B) exactly 3 such triangles} \\quad\\\\ \\text{(C) exactly 7 such triangles} \\quad\\\\ \\text{(D) exactly 15 such triangles} \\quad\\\\ \\text{(E) more than 15 such triangles}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"1052\" height=\"98\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/e/62effaf0d5dc9bfa1d137bed40c4baa73698962d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\fbox{E}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"23\" height=\"23\" />\n</p><p>Take the \"obvious\" equilateral triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2c20f306e46f7431949fe9c321a5e286555e867.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OAP$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the vertex, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is on the upper ray, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is our central point.  Slide <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> down on the top ray to point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/9/1090530ae883b627e090bfb5e450ef796febca40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"16\" />, and slide <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> down an equal distance on the bottom ray to point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f2d56588b9482ec9e17dc73757691f883f6cee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O&#39;$\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Now observe <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/d/24d952486ac9660f468427f8cf95afd4476cfc10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AA&#39;P$\" width=\"62\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d3192407f9a41b9d517f8d14e12ce47d730db0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle OO&#39;P$\" width=\"63\" height=\"14\" />.  We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/8/0787df1776e286de80ba37534dc9fb2e10f3aee5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\angle A = 60^\\circ$\" width=\"90\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/3/6530f781fb30baa6d8526c7e9b404142bd84028e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\angle O = 60^\\circ$\" width=\"91\" height=\"13\" />, therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/c/02c347cdae5c4db2ebe72fb428cee670d8476931.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A \\cong \\angle O$\" width=\"77\" height=\"13\" />.  By our construction of moving the points the same distance, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/6/e3680b5820bc6fcbfe7399dd0fa0b6482ab1b35c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AA&#39; = OO&#39;$\" width=\"88\" height=\"14\" />.  Also, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/9/73931e318bdd61f83468b88108127f12a12ec166.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AP = OP$\" width=\"80\" height=\"13\" /> by the original equilateral triangle.  Therefore, by SAS congruence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/a/dba8d7aa56783b8500494309e4b7ce5e029aea91.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AA&#39;P \\cong \\triangle OO&#39;P$\" width=\"150\" height=\"14\" />.  \n</p><p>Now, look at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/e/70e92430d05d0b25dc46d88dc501c8be01552091.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle A&#39;PO&#39;$\" width=\"66\" height=\"14\" />.  We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/9/64969c8bf628e8684d1dd4fb71367f00e2956f63.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PA&#39; = PO&#39;$\" width=\"89\" height=\"14\" /> from the above congruence.  We also have the included angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/7/6474772536e005d2edf3715f6a620df8f6a76729.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A&#39;PO&#39;$\" width=\"63\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/f/29fa0a9949f018e121b2093216dd5db13d3abef0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" />.  To prove that, start with the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/f/29fa0a9949f018e121b2093216dd5db13d3abef0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/3/5a32ddc4913c683bd00dd7520438eee9f45e093b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$APO$\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" />, subtract the angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/a/a8ad4720f3df43ff082d655ac63d3108aa872aae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$APA&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"16\" />, and add the congruent angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/2/8e26612fb3c496fdd6d3bf551a9a11149156e276.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OPO&#39;$\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/e/70e92430d05d0b25dc46d88dc501c8be01552091.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle A&#39;PO&#39;$\" width=\"66\" height=\"14\" /> is an isosceles triangle with vertex of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/f/29fa0a9949f018e121b2093216dd5db13d3abef0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" />, it is equilateral.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME\" title=\"1993 AHSME\">1993 AHSME</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Problems\" title=\"1993 AHSME Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Answer_Key\" title=\"1993 AHSME Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=44&amp;year=1993\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Problems/Problem_24\" title=\"1993 AHSME Problems/Problem 24\">Problem 24</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Problems/Problem_26\" title=\"1993 AHSME Problems/Problem 26\">Problem 26</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1993 AHSME Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1993 AHSME Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1993 AHSME Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1993 AHSME Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1993 AHSME Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1993 AHSME Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"1993 AHSME Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"1993 AHSME Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1993_AHSME_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"1993 AHSME Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•